FOR VS WHILE LOOPS

In the previous section I aluded to the existance of a loop that is able to run a measured amount of times. This enigmatic code-piece is the for loop. They run code a set amount of times.
They are one of the most quintisential pieces of code when it comes to anything counted &&|| repeated.

For clarity, a standard for loop looks something like this:

  for (i=value;i<value;i++*)
  {
  //code
  }

  *Can also be a retrograde count if needed, or an increment of non-1, such as +2 or +4, whatever fits the code best, so long as it IS a pos/neg modifier.

The differences here stem primarily from the for loops' ability to be measured as I stated previously as a lacking feature of the do-while loop. It's not impossible to measure a do-while loop, but it would
be an unnesesarily convoluted way to do something that the for loop was designed to do by the nature of its components.

You should stick to using do-while loops for scopes or to compartmentalize pieces of code and to have said 'compartments' be de-activated or activated when needed. While for loops remain a top choise for precise
repetition.
